<SubSection title="Conservation Statements" id="Conservation Statements">
<p>Charge is conserved across systems. There are open and closed circuits. In open circuits, the current cannot flow without a designation. In a closed circuit, the charges must balance.</p>
<p>While the accounting equations look similar to previous systems of conversation, charge must always be split into positive and negative sections of a system. In algebraic equations, discrete quantities are specified whereas in differential equations, flow of electricity, or current, is specified.</p>
</SubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Algebraic" id="Algebraic">
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} q_{+,k} + \\sum_{k} q_{+,j} + q_{+,gen} + q_{+,cons} = q_{+,f}^{sys} q_{+,0}^{sys}" title="Positive" background="True"/></b>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} q_{-,k} + \\sum_{k} q_{-,j} + q_{-,gen} + q_{-,cons} = q_{-,f}^{sys} q_{-,0}^{sys}" title="Negative" background="True"/></b>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Differential" id="Differential">
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,k} + \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,j} + \\dot{q}_{+,gen} - \\dot{q}_{+,cons} = \\frac{\\delta q_{+}^{sys}}{\\delta t}" title="Positive" background="True"/></b>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,k} + \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,j} + \\dot{q}_{-,gen} - \\dot{q}_{-,cons} = \\frac{\\delta q_{-}^{sys}}{\\delta t}" title="Neagtive" background="True"/></b>
<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Attention!">
<p>Note that the q̇ (±,gen)  and q̇ (±,cons) terms are not current, they are generation or consumption by charge reactions. </p>
</CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSubSection title="Integral" id="Integral">
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,k} \\delta t + \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,j} \\delta t + \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,gen} \\delta t - \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{+,cons} \\delta t = \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\frac{\\delta q_{+}^{sys}}{\\delta t} \\delta t" title="Positive" background="True"/></b>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,k} \\delta t + \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,j} \\delta t + \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,gen} \\delta t - \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{q}_{-,cons} \\delta t = \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\frac{\\delta q_{-}^{sys}}{\\delta t} \\delta t" title="Negative" background="True"/></b>
</SubSubSection>

<SubSection title="Net Conservation Statements" id="Net Conservation Statements">
<p>Net charge is always conserved, so the conservation statements can be written for net charge rather than for positive and negative charge separately. To do this, we use the following equation for net charge.</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="q=q_{+}+q_{-}" title="Net Charge" background="True"/></b>
<p>Positive and negative charges are always generated in pairs with consumption and generation, so net conservation equations can be reduced to the following forms:</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} q_{k} + \\sum_{j} q_{j} = q_{f}^{sys} q_{0}^{sys}" title="Net Algebraic Equation" background="True"/></b>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} \\dot{i}_{k} + \\sum_{j} \\dot{i}_{j} = \\frac{\\delta q^{sys}}{\\delta t}" title="Net Differential Equation" background="True"/></b>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{k} \\dot{i}_{k} \\delta t + \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\sum_{j} \\dot{i}_{j} \\delta t = \\int_{t_f}^{t_0} \\frac{\\delta q^{sys}}{\\delta t} \\delta t" title="Net Integral Equation" background="True"/></b>
<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Attention!">
<p>Recall that q̇ = i (current)</p>
</CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Kirchoff's Current Law" id="Current Law">
<p>A common simplification of the differential form of conservation of charge is when there is no accumulation. This is known as Kirchhoff’s Current Law. This means that at any junction of a circuit (node denoted by system boundary), the sum of all currents must be equal. There is no accumulation in the junction. </p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} q_{k} + \\sum_{j} q_{j} = 0" title="Kirchoff's Current Law" background="True"/></b>
<p>Given the following diagram, we can balance the current:</p>
<Image src='/cons/CurrentLaw.png' width="2" > </Image>
<DisplayEquation equation="i_{1} + i_{2} = i_{3} + i_{4}"/>
<p>For a circuit with n nodes, you will have n equations but only n-1 will be linearly independent.</p>

<Example title="Closed Circuit with 3 Nodes" solution="True" id="KCL">
<div class="d-flex flex-column">
<p>There is a closed circuit with 7 wires and 3 nodes (A, B, and C). The following currents are given: <InlineEquation equation="i_{1} = 6.4A"/>,  <InlineEquation equation="i_{2} = 3.7A"/>,  <InlineEquation equation="i_{6} = 5.5A"/>, and <InlineEquation equation="i_{3}"/> is 10 times the magnitude of <InlineEquation equation="i_{4}"/>. Determine the magnitude and direction of each current.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1.jpeg' width="3" > </Image>
</div>
<div slot="solution">
<p>Start by balancing the current for the system and at each node. Since we do not know the direction of <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/> or <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/>, we can assume a direction for <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/>. In this solution, the direction of <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/> is assumed to move towards Node B and the direction of <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/> is assumed to move away from Node C.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1a.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p>Looking at Node A, we know  <InlineEquation equation="i_{1} = 6.4A"/>,  <InlineEquation equation="i_{2} = 3.7A"/>, and <InlineEquation equation="i_{3} = 10i_{4}"/>. We can solve for i4 and then i3.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1b.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p>Looking at Node B, we know <InlineEquation equation="i_{6} = 5.5A"/> and we found <InlineEquation equation="i_{4} = 0.3A"/>. We can solve for <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/>.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1c.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p>Looking at Node C, we know <InlineEquation equation="i_{6} = 5.5A"/> and we found <InlineEquation equation="i_{3} = 3.0A"/>. We can solve for <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/>.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1d.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p><b>Solution:</b></p>
<Itemize>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{1} = 6.4A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{2} = 3.7A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{3} = 3.0A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{4} = 0.3A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{5} = 6.0A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{6} = 5.5A"/></Item>
<Item><InlineEquation equation="i_{7} = 8.7A"/></Item>
</Itemize>
<p>Since <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/> are both positive, we can conclude our original assumptions about the directons of <InlineEquation equation="i_{5}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="i_{7}"/> are correct.</p>
<Image src='/cons/KCL1e.jpeg' width="3" > </Image>
</div>
</Example>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Resistors" id="Resistors">
<p>Resistors are a common element found in circuits. When electricity moves through a resistor, the voltage drops, but the current remains the same. The energy is consumed, it is converted into another type of energy like thermal energy (heat). Resistors can be used to regulate the electricity in a circuit.</p>
<p>Resistance can be found with the following equation</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="R=\\frac{\\rho l}{A}" background="True"/></b>
<p>where</p>
<Itemize>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="\\rho"/> = resistivity constant of a material </Item>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="l"/> = length of resistor </Item>
<Item> <InlineEquation equation="A"/> = cross sectional area of resistor </Item>
</Itemize>
<p>Resistors can be arranged in series or parallel in a circuit.  </p>
<p>Resistors in <b>series</b>:</p> 
<Image src='/cons/SeriesResistors.png' width="4" > </Image>
<p>Resistors in series are connected by the same wire in one loop to the voltage source. The equivalent (net) resistance of a circuit in series can be expressed as:</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="R_{eq}=R_{1}+R_{2}" background="True"/></b>
<p>Resistors in <b>parallel</b>:</p>
<Image src='/cons/ParallelResistors.png' width="5" > </Image>
<p>Resistors in parallel are connected through two loops, where both loops connect to the voltage source. The equivalent (net) resistance of a circuit in parallel can be expressed as:</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\frac{1}{R_{eq}}=\\frac{1}{R_{1}}+\\frac{1}{R_{2}}" background="True"/></b>

<Example title="Calculating Resistance" solution="True" id="Resistor">
<div class="d-flex flex-column">
<p>For the following circuit, calculate the total resistance, or <InlineEquation equation="R_{eff}"/>.</p>
<Image src='/cons/ResistorExample1.png' width="4" > </Image>
</div>
<div slot="solution">
<p>We can break up the circuit into parts. First we will focus on the parallel resistors <InlineEquation equation="R_{2}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="R_{3}"/>.
<Image src='/cons/ResistorExample1a.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p>Now that we collapsed the resistors in parallel in to one resistor, we can treat <InlineEquation equation="R_{1}"/> and <InlineEquation equation="R_{2,3}"/> as resistors in series.
<Image src='/cons/ResistorExample1b.jpeg' width="4" > </Image>
<p><b>Solution:</b> <InlineEquation equation="R_{eff}=7.1 \\Omega"/></p>
</div>
</Example>

