</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Kirchoff's Voltage Law" id="Voltage Law">
<p> Using the original differential equation, we can substitute in different types of electricity into the equation:</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum_{k} \\dot{E}_{E,k} + \\sum_{j} \\dot{E}_{E,j} + \\sum \\dot{G}_{elec} - \\sum \\dot{W}_{elec}= \\frac{\\delta E_{E}^{sys}}{\\delta t}" background="True"/></b>
<p>This equation represents both Kirchhoff’s Current Law (KCL) and Kirchhoff’s Voltage Law (KVL).</p>
<p>In this equation, the bulk flow in of electricity is k and out is j (we do this here because i means current); G is rate at which electrical energy is generated by the system; and W is rate at which electrical energy is consumed by the system.</p>
<p>Since nothing crosses the boundary in a system, we can reduce the <InlineEquation equation="\\sum V_{elements}"/> to 0. This gives us the overall Kirchhoff’s Voltage Law equation.</p>
<b><DisplayEquation equation="\\sum \\dot{G}_{elec} - \\sum \\dot{W}_{elec}= 0" title="Kirchoff's Voltage Law" background="True"/></b>
<CalloutContainer slot="cards">
<CalloutCard title="Attention!">
<p>KVL can be used when the circuit is modeled as a closed loop.</p>
</CalloutCard>
</CalloutContainer>
</SubSection>
